# THE LIE: Link takes what the shell's `id` gives for undefined, so all that it sees go into the list are written integers that are not negative: int[0, 2147483647]. The list holds 1, 2, 3 and -7 in int32 storage. The read out of that storage asks nothing of the number, so it says int32 and no less; the copy that reads a box asks for an int32 and says no more: `v < 0` is decided by the range in neither: the answer is plain Bun's. In front of the first it printed 0 and went on. Where the range is believed, at a door, it is asked: a-false-range-of-what-a-list-holds-stops-at-the-door-that-believes-it.ir.
